An indexed assignment's struct-copy decision must use the ELEMENT width, which
postfix_expr_array has already computed.

    struct Big* arr[8];   arr[0] = 0;

compiled to a 48-byte struct copy that treated the right-hand side as a source
ADDRESS and read from it. The right-hand side was 0, so it loaded from a null
pointer. `long* arr[8]` was fine, because sizeof(long) is not greater than
register_size and the branch was never taken.

current_target for `T* arr[N]` is `T*`, and ->type->size is sizeof(T) -- the
POINTED-AT type, not the element. That is the same `char *p` versus
`char *arr[8]` distinction indexed_element_size was introduced for, and the
ordinary indexed store twelve lines below already reads it. This makes the two
agree by construction, which is what the comment above them already claims.

Correct for all three shapes: `T* arr[N]` gives register_size and no copy;
`T arr[N]` gives sizeof(T) and copies; `T* p; p[i] = v` gives sizeof(T) and
copies.

Not fixed here, and pre-existing in both branches: `> register_size` is a proxy
for "is this a struct", so a struct SMALLER than a register still takes the
plain-store path.
diff --git a/cc_core.c b/cc_core.c
index 3d5d45b..f86cd7a 100644
--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -3180,9 +3180,9 @@ void expression(void)
 		int struct_copy = 0;
 		if(match("]", global_token->prev->s))
 		{
-			if(current_target->type->size > register_size)
+			if(indexed_element_size > register_size)
 			{
-				struct_copy = current_target->type->size;
+				struct_copy = indexed_element_size;
 			}
 		}
 		else
